{"step_index":850,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T08:11:08Z","thinking":"`ErrorController.Unauthorized()` is not protected by an authorization attribute, leading to the `Unauthorized.cshtml` view being rendered within the layout. The layout includes the menu, retrieving user permissions, which explains the header and navbar, but the main body shows a 403 error. The root cause for the 403 error is authorization failing, and it is necessary to determine why the `IsAuthorize` function returned `false`.\n\n\nThe authorization check within `AuthorizeAttribute.cs` uses `user.UserPermission` to determine access, specifically querying based on `APIAction` and `APIController`. The `user.UserPermission` data is populated within `UserPrincipal.cs` by retrieving permissions from a `Permission.Data` structure, filtering based on role IDs. This suggests that permission assignment relies on roles and API action/controller mappings.\n\n\nDuring session save, `UserPrincipal` including `UserPermission` is serialized using a serialization library because the property lacks an explicit ignore attribute.  The deserialization in `Load` attempts to reconstruct `UserPrincipal` from the session data, potentially restoring the `UserPermission` data. If there's corruption during token deserialization, an empty principal is effectively loaded. The `UserPermission` data is obtained within the `UserPrincipal`, so the next step is determining how this is populated during the save.\n\n\nThe `PermissionRes` class's serialization behavior using the Newtonsoft library has been investigated; only the `RoleFunctionName` property is serialized due to the `JsonIgnore` attribute on the other fields. The saved session data will therefore only include a list of `RoleFunctionName` strings within the `UserPermission` array. Deserialization of the `UserPrincipal` object during session loading is performed using the `JsonConvert.DeserializeObject` method.\n\n\nDuring the deserialization of the `UserPrincipal`, the constructor is called first, initializing the object but leaving a critical internal variable (`_roleIDs`) as null. The serialization library then attempts to deserialize the `UserPermission` property, which lacks a setter. This triggers a call to the getter, which in turn invokes a method to retrieve the permissions. Within that method, a null check on `_roleIDs` prevents the permission data from being populated, leading to the authorization failure. The issue stems from the order of operations in deserialization and the use of a null variable within a permission retrieval.\n\n\nAfter deserialization, the `_userPermission` list within the `UserPrincipal` object is populated with permission entries, but the deserialization process sets `APIController` and `APIAction` to null. Subsequently, the `RestoreTokens` method updates the `_roleIDs`. When authorization is checked, the null `APIController` and `APIAction` values cause the authorization to fail because the controller and action are not matched. This results in redirection to the unauthorized page. The incorrect authorization stems from the initial deserialization process failing to correctly populate these permission details.\n\n\nIn the older MVC5 system, the in-memory session stored the complete `UserPrincipal` object directly, without serialization. Permission data was lazily loaded.  The current issue arises because the new system uses JSON-based session storage and the `UserPrincipal` now requires serialization/deserialization.\n\nThe crucial difference is that the old system's `[ScriptIgnore]` attributes had *no* effect on the in-memory session data, but the new system is using those values for serialization, changing the way the application loads session information. The new code attempts to reconstruct the object, but the lazy-loading behavior creates a dependency that's not correctly initialized during deserialization, which causes the authorization failure.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"e:/Work/Place_Migrate/cci-store-location-web/01_origin/CoC.Web.Place/Views/Shared/_Scripts.cshtml\"","EndLine":"100","StartLine":"70","toolAction":"\"Viewing 01_origin _Scripts.cshtml\"","toolSummary":"\"View 01_origin _Scripts.cshtml\""}}]}
