{"step_index":864,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-29T08:12:24Z","thinking":"Investigating `UserPrincipal.cs`, specifically the `RestoreTokens` method within the session-specific overlay. Considering how this method handles token and role ID restoration. The focus is on understanding the role it plays in managing user secrets.\n\n\nThe `RestoreTokens` method clears `_userPermission` and `_module`. The getter for `UserPermission` and `Module` now checks if `_roleIDs` is null before fetching permissions. The original implementation could have led to a scenario where permissions would not be re-fetched after token restoration if `_roleIDs` was null, leading to potential security vulnerabilities. Now, a defensive null check ensures that permissions are not fetched when `_roleIDs` is null, preventing unexpected behavior and improving security.\n\n\n`UserSessionStore.Save` serializes the `UserPrincipal`, including `UserPermission`, storing it in the session. Deserialization during `UserSessionStore.Load` initially populates a temporary `UserPermission` list due to `_roleIDs` being null. Subsequent calls to `UserPermission` after `RestoreTokens` correctly fetch and update the full permission list. Serializing the `UserPrincipal` on page rendering includes the correct permissions from the updated `UserPermission`.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"grep_search","args":{"CaseInsensitive":"false","MatchPerLine":"true","Query":"\"PlaceHome.Read\"","SearchPath":"\"e:/Work/Place_Migrate/cci-store-location-web\"","toolAction":"\"Searching for PlaceHome.Read\"","toolSummary":"\"Search PlaceHome.Read\""}}]}
