{"step_index":2556,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-30T03:33:21Z","thinking":"The access token's expiration is expected behavior for this authentication scheme. Investigating the communication between the web and API components on the staging/dev environment is the next logical step. The target API is built on .NET 10.\n\n\nIt's confirmed that the .NET 10 API utilizes `System.Text.Json` natively. The need for the object runtime type serialization in the client stems from how `System.Text.Json` serializes a generic `object` into an empty JSON object (`{}`). Without the client-side type information, this empty object is passed to the API, leading to an empty DTO being bound and bypassing the API logic.\n\n\nThe potential for passing null values to the database through the SQL statement requires ensuring the client sends the correct type information during serialization, preventing empty objects from being passed. Further investigation is needed into the implications of using PascalCase versus camelCase in .NET 10 API, specifically concerning compatibility with the legacy API on Staging 4 and potential impact on DTOs. Despite .NET 10's flexibility in accepting both, the Staging 4 environment might have strict casing requirements.\n\n\nPascalCase serialization using `PropertyNamingPolicy = null` ensures compatibility across both .NET Framework 4.8 and .NET 10 environments, preventing Staging 4 failures. The observed .NET 10 API hosting under IIS suggests that chunked transfer encoding, even though natively supported by the web server, could be subject to buffering due to IIS configurations.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"grep_search","args":{"CaseInsensitive":"true","Includes":"[\"*.cs\"]","MatchPerLine":"true","Query":"\"JsonSerializerOptions\"","SearchPath":"\"e:\\\\Work\\\\Place_Migrate\\\\cci-store-location-api\\\\02_target\"","toolAction":"\"Searching JsonSerializerOptions\"","toolSummary":"\"Search JsonSerializerOptions in API target\""}}]}
