{"step_index":3037,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-30T08:06:21Z","thinking":"Initial observation: The Kestrel web server is configured to serve static files exclusively from the `wwwroot` directory. Previous migration decisions involved moving content, data, and a script handler into this directory. The `content/DriveJson` directory contains a sensitive `client_secret.json` file.\n\n\nThe `content/DriveJson` directory's contents are unreachable over HTTP after the `wwwroot` move due to the absence of a MIME map for JSON files in the IIS configuration, while `UseStaticFiles` configuration serves every known extension. A case-insensitive static file path rewriter is needed to support Linux Docker containers where file systems are case-sensitive. The initial approach involves creating an index of files within the `wwwroot` directory using case-insensitive comparison.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"replace_file_content","args":{"AllowMultiple":"false","Description":"\"Add case-insensitive static file path resolver for Linux containers\"","EndLine":"155","Instruction":"\"Add static file dictionary and path rewrite middleware before Core.MVC registers UseStaticFiles\"","ReplacementContent":"\"            //AI[MIGRATE_START]: Kestrel serves static files only from wwwroot (content/, data/, script-handler/ moved there verbatim - migration-decisions D14)\\n            // content/DriveJson holds a Google OAuth client_secret.json that only commented-out code ever read (GoogleDriveController); IIS served no .json without a mimeMap in Web.config, while UseStaticFiles serves every known extension, so this folder stays unreachable over HTTP after the wwwroot move.\\n            // MUST be registered BEFORE HostBuilderItem.ApplicationBuilderItem because Core.MVC registers its own UseStaticFiles() internally.\\n            app.Use(async (context, next) =>\\n            {\\n                if (context.Request.Path.StartsWithSegments(\\\"