rehensive test execution capability. Investigation into the `playwright.config.ts` file revealed timeout and retry settings, which are relevant for test stability, but worker count is not e\n<truncated 228 bytes>\n, though most tests are read-only. The number of worker processes in the Playwright configuration should also be examined; the current setup suggests an optimal worker count for performance optimization during testing. The current system default is half of the available CPU cores, though a specific number of workers might be set for faster test execution.\n\nFurther analysis reveals the user's requirement to integrate web test data into a central portal, which initially seems distinct from the local web dashboard. The local dashboard is separate from the central portal designed for API test results, potentially involving different databases and data ingestion methods. Thus, understanding data compatibility is crucial, particularly if the user is expecting data to insert into this portal.\n\n\nThe `upload-report.js` script, intended for the API dashboard, requires a database connection and cloud storage credentials, specifically a bearer token.  The web application's authentication method, via browser cookies and Keycloak, doesn't provide this required bearer token. Consequently, the script's core functionality that fetches a signed URL using a token will fail. The difference in authentication methods between the web and API applications prevents the direct reuse of the upload script without modification. I must determine how the API authenticates to Keycloak, and how that relates to the web app.\n\n\nThe user's intent to run all tests and target either `stg10`, `stg4`, or both environments needs clarification. Given the test count, I should confirm if a full suite run is desired immediately, or a background execution with progress updates is more appropriate, while assessing the feasibility of running on all stages. The local web report mechanism is distin