{"step_index":3810,"source":"MODEL","type":"SEARCH_WEB","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-01T08:30:53Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-01T15:30:53+07:00\nCompleted At: 2026-10-01T15:30:57+07:00\nThe search for \"\"Replace legacy time zone\" DBeaver\" returned the following summary:\nTo resolve issues where DBeaver encounters errors related to \"legacy time zones\" (often manifesting as `FATAL: invalid value for parameter \"TimeZone\"` when connecting to databases like PostgreSQL), you can use the **Replace legacy time zone** setting[1][2].\n\n### How to Enable \"Replace Legacy Time Zone\"\n1.  Open DBeaver and locate your database connection in the **Database Navigator**.\n2.  Right-click the connection and select **Edit Connection**.\n3.  Navigate to **Advanced** > **Settings**[1][3].\n4.  Find the option labeled **Replace legacy time zone** and ensure it is **checked/turned on**[1][4].\n5.  Click **OK** and reconnect to your database.\n\n### Why this happens\nThis error typically occurs because of a mismatch between the time zone database versions used by your Java Virtual Machine (JVM) and your database server[5][6]. For example, older time zone names like `Asia/Calcutta` are often considered \"legacy\" or obsolete and have been replaced by names like `Asia/Kolkata`[5][7]. When the JDBC driver attempts to set the session time zone using the JVM's default, the database may reject the obsolete name[5][6]. Enabling this setting allows the driver to handle these legacy names more gracefully[1].\n\n**Note:** If the connection issue persists, ensure your database server and client environment are updated to the latest time zone data files. In some cases, configuring your JVM to use a specific time zone (e.g., `-Duser.timezone=UTC` in your `dbeaver.ini` file) can also help stabilize time-related behavior[1][8].\n\nSources:\n[1] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHXM6Lt4H9btJ9fqn0xmYlUdsCrp-uPIeaI7J8W0ZPSSDtSlTWsu