{"step_index":5535,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-06T09:37:18Z","thinking":"The files consistently utilize UTF-8 with BOM; this encoding must be preserved during modifications. The focus shifts to comparing `APIClient.cs` between the \"Place\" and \"DC\" contexts. Specifically, the JSON serialization options are under examination, particularly the `PropertyNamingPolicy`.\n\n\nInvestigating the `Post` and `Put` methods in `APIClient.cs`, I see that generic and object-based overloads are present. The JSON serialization utilizes a shared `_jsonOptions` instance. It seems the `PropertyNameCaseInsensitive` setting is enabled; this setting may influence the expected data format.\n\n\nAnalyzing the `Put` methods in `APIClient.cs`, both generic and object-based versions are present; they rely on the same `JsonBody` serialization. This shared serialization likely uses the same `_jsonOptions`, which includes `PropertyNameCaseInsensitive`.\n\n\nThe original issue stemmed from `chunked` transfer encoding and camelCase names sent by the new serialization, causing a 500 error on the API side due to a null request object binding. The `PostObject` method with `object` parameters was likely the source of this problem.\n\nBy switching to Newtonsoft JSON serialization and changing to use the generic `Post` method in `ApiHelper.cs`, the issue is resolved by ensuring a buffered body with PascalCase names. The corrected serialization with `System.Text.Json` serializes all properties correctly due to the dynamic type resolution.\n\n\nExamining `Startup.cs` in the specified project, it appears `AddNewtonsoftJson` is used for JSON serialization, and there is a concern about ensuring PascalCase naming consistency with `System.Text.Json` elsewhere. Hypothesis: adding `AddJsonOptions` might provide global control over `System.Text.Json` serialization settings, specifically property naming. Further investigation required to assess the safety and appropriateness of this addition.\n\n\nAdding both `.AddJsonOptions` and `.AddNewtonsoftJson` ensures consistent PascalCase naming regardless of the chosen serializer. A previous test's explicit configuration suggests property names are intentionally left unchanged, contradicting this approach.\n\nThe functional tests failed due to a missing views assembly (`CoC.Web.SCM.Views2.dll`) not being copied to the test's output directory. The test's base directory is where the views assembly should be. Now, let's explore if including the views assembly solves the functional test failures.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"Test-Path \\\"02_target/CoC.Web.SCM/bin/Debug/net10.0/CoC.Web.SCM.Views2.dll\\\"\"","Cwd":"\"e:\\\\Work\\\\Place_Migrate\\\\cci-scm-web\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Checking CoC.Web.SCM.Views2.dll exists\"","toolSummary":"\"Check Views2.dll in main bin\""}}]}
