{"step_index":5550,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-06T09:38:19Z","thinking":"The successful JSON tests confirm the base functionality. The focus now shifts to `cci-scm-web` to check for similar PascalCase/camelCase inconsistencies encountered in the `place` project. The strategy is to replicate the fixes applied to `place` if analogous issues are identified in `cci-scm-web`.\n\n\nInitial assessment suggests the serialization issue stems from the default `.NET 10` behavior `JsonSerializerDefaults.Web`, which mandates `camelCase` property names conflicting with the backend's `PascalCase` expectation. The observed `chunked` encoding also likely causes the server compression handler to crash. The \"place\" project successfully addressed these problems by overriding the serializer options.\n\n\nExamining the codebase, the identified pattern in `place` and `dc-web` suggests a potential resolution in `scm-web`. The existing API client in `scm-web` uses `PostObject` and `PutObject` which might exhibit the same serialization issue. The hypothesis is to refactor these to generic methods like `Post<T>` and `Put<T>` to manage the serialization, following the successful approach from the other projects. This refactoring will allow explicit type handling, which should resolve the PascalCase/camelCase problem.\n\n\nThe `scm-web` project's reliance on a different JSON library and inconsistent use of serialization options deviates from the standardized approach of `place` and `dc-web`. The existing implementation uses `Newtonsoft.Json` and lacks proper disposal of `StringContent`, suggesting memory leak and a deviation from the established pattern. The `PostMethod` in the API helper incorrectly calls `PostObject` instead of the generic `Post` method, introducing further inconsistency.\n\nThe solution requires aligning `scm-web` with `place` and `dc-web`. This involves replacing the current `Newtonsoft.Json` usage with the .NET 10 nati