{"step_index":5898,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-07T03:40:45Z","thinking":"Examining the `BonusOutputSalesController.cs` file, specifically the `Search` method, to understand its functionality. The presence of `MvcAuthorizeAttribute(AuthorizeAction: false)` suggests an authorization bypass, which requires careful review of its implications. The controller appears to handle search requests for bonus output sales, indicated by the `BonusOutputSaleSearchReq` parameter.\n\n\nIf the input data is invalid, the process returns an error immediately. Valid search requests proceed with processing, adjusting customer ID and search key parameters to ensure they are properly handled by the internal logic. The system then attempts to retrieve search results via a POST request to an external resource. A successful response is handled by packaging the returned data; any exception during this process results in an error status.\n\n\nThe error handling logic might not catch errors if the external resource call itself succeeds, but an error status is set internally within the call's method. Reviewing the POST method implementation reveals that error statuses might be set within this method. The process appears to set the status code of the operation internally. It seems that there may be a case missed when setting the status code, even when the call does not throw.\n\n\nThe external resource call's error status might be overwritten if data deserialization succeeds, even if the HTTP status code indicates an error. Specifically, a 500 response from the external resource might result in a successful deserialization (e.g., to a default `PagingResponse`), which then incorrectly sets the internal status to `Success`. This contradicts the AngularJS code's handling of the error status. It is crucial to determine how the external call's error status gets propagated to the AngularJS front-end. It is important to confirm whether the exception handling in the controller correctly handles the status.\n\n\nAn error condition or null request parameter triggers error logging. Initial focus is on creating a local file logger to capture detailed information about these errors. The target log file path and the specific data to be logged are defined, including timestamps, caller information, and request/response details.\n\nConsidering whether to create a dedicated helper class or integrate the logger into an existing helper class for broader use across the controller's methods. The logging strategy should be generalized to include all relevant controller methods.\n\n\nThe logging mechanism utilizes a file-based approach within the application's directory to capture detailed information including timestamps and user context. This logger is designed to be resilient, ensuring that errors within the logging process itself do not cause application failure.  The log file's location is being constructed using the base application directory and a predefined subdirectory.\n\n\nThe code checks for a null request, returning an error if present. Valid requests adjust parameters, such as customer ID and search key, before making a POST request. Upon a successful call to the external resource, the internal status may be set to \"Success\" even if the API call reports an error through its HTTP status.\n\nThe logging mechanism is triggered for the null request condition, or when errors occur. It logs the API request and response data, which will include the total and count of the records.\n\n\nAn exception handler logs detailed exception information, including stack traces and inner exceptions, to a local debug logger. The error status is set on an object and includes HTTP status code details. The logging targets errors encountered in the `Search` method of the controller.\n\n\nThe `ApiHelper.PostMethod` logs API calls and their status.  The logging occurs before and after the external resource call. Any exception during the external API call in `BonusOutputSalesController.Search` is caught and logged, which includes exception details and stack traces.  Given the incorrect API URL, a \"connection refused\" error is highly likely.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"git log -n 5 -p -- appsettings.json\"","Cwd":"\"e:\\\\Work\\\\Place_Migrate\\\\cci-scm-web\\\\02_target\\\\CoC.Web.SCM\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Running command\"","toolSummary":"\"Git log for appsettings.json\""}}]}
