{"step_index":6160,"source":"MODEL","type":"VIEW_FILE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-07T04:29:09Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-07T11:29:09+07:00\nCompleted At: 2026-10-07T11:29:09+07:00\nFile Path: `file:///e:/Work/Place_Migrate/cci-dc-web/02_target/Makefile`\nTotal Lines: 115\nTotal Bytes: 4796\nShowing lines 20 to 50\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n20: LIST_PIDS = lsof -ti:$(PORT) -sTCP:LISTEN\n21: KILL_PID  = kill -9\n22: PROC_NAME = ps -p $$pid -o comm=\n23: endif\n24: \n25: KILL_BY_PORT = for p in $$($(LIST_PIDS) 2>/dev/null); do $(KILL_PID) $$p >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; done\n26: \n27: PROJECT_DIR := CoC.Web.DC\n28: CSPROJ      := $(PROJECT_DIR)/CoC.Web.DC.csproj\n29: SLN         := CoC.Web.DC.sln\n30: # Port = app.config <runPorts app=\"target\"> (5311)\n31: PORT        ?= 5311\n32: HEALTH_PATH ?= /\n33: # Prefer bare `dotnet` on PATH (avoids \"/c/Program Files/...\" space breakage in recipes)\n34: DOTNET      ?= dotnet\n35: \n36: OPEN       ?= 1\n37: OPEN_DELAY ?= 4\n38: ifeq ($(OS),Windows_NT)\n39: OPEN_URL = powershell.exe -NoProfile -Command \"Start-Process 'http://localhost:$(PORT)/'\"\n40: else\n41: OPEN_URL = (command -v xdg-open >/dev/null 2>&1 && xdg-open \"http://localhost:$(PORT)/\" || open \"http://localhost:$(PORT)/\")\n42: endif\n43: \n44: VERIFY_LOG  := /tmp/dc-web-$(PORT)-verify.log\n45: VERIFY_PID  := /tmp/dc-web-$(PORT)-verify.pid\n46: \n47: help:\n48: \t@echo \"Available targets (port $(PORT)):\"\n49: \t@echo \"  kill-port / restore / build / run / start / dev / verify / test\"\n50: \t@echo \"Bom config o root: (cd .. && make config)\"\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":6161,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-07T04:29:09Z","thinking":"Analyzing the `appsettings.json` file to understand the current configuration. Each field within the `AppSettings` section is being reviewed, including the `ClientSecret`, `TrustedSSLCert`, file upload paths, and logging configurations. Examining the details of each configuration element to identify any potential issues or areas for further investigation.\n\n\nThe configuration indicates Vault is disabled, with corresponding API endpoints and secrets defined. Investigating the various service URLs to understand dependencies and communication patterns within the application. Noticed specific configurations for logging, environment setup, and client details, pointing to a staging environment with elastic logging enabled.\n\n\nExamining the application's service URLs reveals dependencies on staging and UAT environments, essential for understanding the deployment context. Now analyzing the `cci.core.json` file for more concrete configurations, particularly authentication, logging, and caching settings. The Keycloak identity provider is configured with specific OAuth2 client details and scope.\n\nThe logging configuration points to a Kibana instance. Redis caching is currently disabled, however, the Redis connection string details are provided.\n\n\nThe Keycloak settings define the identity provider URLs, issuers, and audiences.  Observing discrepancies in the Kibana logging configuration between the target and deployed configurations is crucial. Comparing the logging configurations, the `vaults-gitops` and `dc-web-mssql` environments' Kibana settings match, but the target environment differs.\n\n\nAnalyzing the client scopes and audiences defined in the `cci.core.json` files for different services, specifically focusing on `dc-web-mssql` and `scm-web-mssql` environments.  The presence of Kibana logging is confirmed by inspecting the `StartupDependencyRegistrar.cs` file, which references `IsUseKibana` from the application configuration.  Confirming the value of `IsUseElasticLogger` from `appsettings.json`, and deducing `AppConfig.IsUseKibana` must therefore be true.\n\n\nRedis isn't required based on the configuration file, so that's likely not the issue. The application startup code includes a check for a missing compiled views DLL, which, if absent, will cause a crash due to a file-not-found exception. This could manifest as a 503 error from the ingress.\n\nAlso, the target port configuration needs to be verified in the Dockerfile, as a misconfigured port would also cause external access issues.\n\n\nThe Kestrel server in the container is configured to listen on port 5316. The `dc-web` service exposes port 5311, which may indicate a port mismatch. Reviewing the historical context, the previous port used by `scm-web` was 5602, and the correct port is 5316 in the current setup.\n\nThe ingress routing is likely misconfigured, potentially directing traffic to port 5602 (or 80/5000), while the application now listens on 5316, resulting in a 503 error. Investigate the `ClientUrl` configuration within `appsettings.json` to ensure consistency.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"E:\\\\Work\\\\dev-ops\\\\vaults-gitops\\\\.gitlab-ci.yml\"","EndLine":"100","StartLine":"1","toolAction":"\"Viewing file\"","toolSummary":"\"View .gitlab-ci.yml in vaults-gitops\""}}]}
