{"step_index":7047,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-09T03:19:03Z","thinking":"Investigating how ASP.NET Core handles form data binding to a complex object parameter, specifically, whether `Reqs` could be null. Considering a scenario where the request body is JSON but lacks the `[FromBody]` attribute on the action method parameter. This scenario suggests a potential for null binding, which requires further verification.\n\n\nASP.NET Core, without `[FromBody]`, binds the `Reqs` parameter from form values when the request is `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, as sent by jQuery. The framework's default `JQueryFormValueProvider` handles the square bracket notation used by jQuery, correctly mapping `Reqs[PageIndex]` to `Reqs.PageIndex`. The `PostMethod` call is traced to an API endpoint on a staging environment.\n\nThe staging API (`scm-api-stg4.concung.vn`) returned a 204 No Content response, which will require further investigation to determine if this is the expected behavior and why.\n\n\nUpon receiving a 204 HTTP status code, `ApiHelper.cs` calls `SetErrorAPI`.  The `SetErrorAPI` method checks if the status code is 200 or 201; if not, it sets the status to \"Error\", records the HTTP status code, and constructs an error message. The error message includes the status code, its corresponding text, and the API endpoint.\n\n\nLine 656, attempting to resolve an API client, could throw an exception. The root cause lies within how the application's service container is configured and whether the requested `IAPIClient` has been successfully registered and resolved, in this case it is Autofac and HttpClient. Examining the dependency registration, the `HttpClient` and `APIClient` implementations are registered.\n\n\nAutofac registers both `HttpClient` and `APIClient` as `InstancePerLifetimeScope`. Resolving these from the root container creates new instances. The code defensively checks for a potentially null `ContainerManager` in multiple locations, including `VaultHelper.cs` and `MvcAuthorizeAttribute.cs`.\n\nHowever, `StartupSubcribeMessageFromRedis.cs` directly resolves a service from `ContainerManager`, which would throw an exception at startup if `ContainerManager` were indeed null. Thus the suspicion that it *is* null can be discarded. Further analysis is needed.\n```\n\nIf case 204 in `SetErrorAPI` were executed, the `_CodeStep.Message` could not be null, because it would be assigned a specific non-null message. The fact that the caught exception in `OutputSaleCharitiesController.cs` has a null message suggests that case 204 was never reached within `SetErrorAPI`. This implies that either the `PostMethod` was never called, or the response status code was not 204.\n\n\nThe observed behavior on STG4, where a 204 response results in a \"Warning\" status, provides a crucial comparison point. The key difference on STG10 is the null `_ObjApiHelper.objCodeStep.Message` causing the crash, despite an expected 204 response. Three hypotheses exist: an exception before message assignment, a non-204 response from `PostMethod`, or a null `Reqs` parameter. Each possibility necessitates further investigation.\n\n\nExamining the controller code, a null `Reqs` parameter would result in a `NullReferenceException` at line 61, inside the `try` block, followed by another in the `catch` block on line 73.  This pattern leads to an unhandled exception, resulting in HTTP 500. The frontend JavaScript constructs the search object, indicating form data is being posted.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"dotnet --version\"","Cwd":"\"E:/Work/Place_Migrate/cci-scm-web\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Checking dotnet version\"","toolSummary":"\"Check dotnet version\""}}]}
