{"step_index":7075,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-09T03:21:48Z","thinking":"Initial investigation focuses on `appSetting.config` within the .NET Framework, specifically targeting the `aspnet:MaxHttpCollectionKeys` setting, which may require adjustment in IIS, machine-level configuration, or web.config. Further analysis reveals an ASP.NET MVC limitation tied to the jQuery data structure `Reqs[StoreIDs][]=1572`, where a key limit of 1000 applies. This setting can be customized in machine.config or the registry within IIS.\n\n\nASP.NET Core's `FormOptions` define limits on form data; specifically, the default `ValueCountLimit` is 1024. Exceeding this limit results in an `InvalidOperationException` during model binding, causing the bound parameter (`Reqs` in this case) to remain null. This null value then propagates through the `Search` method, potentially leading to errors.\n\n\nThe initial `NullReferenceException` when `Reqs` is null is handled, but a subsequent, uncaught exception within the `catch` block arises when accessing a potentially null message property. The issue stems from the assumption that the message property is always initialized. This second exception propagates upward, resulting in the 500 error. The fix requires checking for null before using the message property or ensuring it is initialized in all cases.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"grep_search","args":{"CaseInsensitive":"true","MatchPerLine":"true","Query":"\"FormOptions\"","SearchPath":"\"E:/Work/Place_Migrate/cci-scm-web/02_target\"","toolAction":"\"Searching for FormOptions in 02_target\"","toolSummary":"\"Check FormOptions in 02_target\""}}]}
