{"step_index":7782,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-09T04:23:29Z","thinking":"The `CurrentUser` property attempts to cast the generic `User` object to a `UserPrincipal`. The `ChildActionViewComponent` creates a controller instance using dependency injection and constructs route data for the action. This suggests a user context is being established and then passed to a child action through routing. This setup might be involved in user authorization within a specific application context.\n\n\nThe child action constructs a controller context, and its `HttpContext` is the same as the parent's, implying inherited user context. The crucial point is authorization attributes are NOT applied to the error controller. This means the `HttpContext.User` is likely *not* set during an unauthorized access. The cookie authentication middleware remains the likely source of the `User` object.\n\n\nThe `ErrorController.Unauthorized()` method receives an unauthenticated `HttpContext.User`. Since `ErrorController` lacks the authorization attribute, `httpContext.User` isn't assigned the `UserPrincipal`. The layout then retrieves the `UserPrincipal` from the session via `MvcAuthorizeAttribute.ReadSessionUser()`. This leads to the child action being invoked with user information from the session, not the unauthenticated context.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"e:/Work/Place_Migrate/cci-scm-web/02_target/CoC.Web.SCM/Views/Home/LoadUserLogin.cshtml\"","toolAction":"\"Viewing LoadUserLogin.cshtml\"","toolSummary":"\"View LoadUserLogin.cshtml\""}}]}
