{"step_index":7840,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-09T04:26:14Z","thinking":"`HomeController` utilizes a property `CurrentUser` that casts the `User` object to `UserPrincipal`, potentially returning null. This null check is critical, since a null `CurrentUser` is possible. `LoadUserLogin(int UserID)` function utilizes the `MvcDonutOutputCacheAttribute`.\n\n\nThe code in `getMenuChild` accesses `CurrentUser` without a null check, specifically within a loop that checks permissions. A `NullReferenceException` will be thrown if `CurrentUser` is null during the `IsPermission` check. The issue likely stems from how `CurrentUser` is populated in the `LoadUserLogin` action, as it could return null.\n\n\nThe `ErrorController`'s `Unauthorized` action isn't subject to the authorization filter, so the `httpContext.User` isn't assigned. Consequently, when `HomeController.Menu()` is invoked through the layout, `CurrentUser` will be null within the controller's context. This causes the potential `NullReferenceException`. The layout correctly loads `CurrentUser` from session, thus, `CurrentUser` is not null in the layout file.\n\n\nThe unhandled exception in `Menu` is caught by the exception handler, which then re-renders the layout, triggering `Menu` again, leading to a loop and a 500 error. The `LoadUserLogin` action, invoked in the layout, also receives the potentially null `CurrentUser`, but handles this gracefully with a null check in the view. It's essential to verify other controller actions called by the layout to find potential null reference issues.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"grep_search","args":{"CaseInsensitive":"true","MatchPerLine":"true","Query":"\"ApiEndPointOutputCacheData\"","SearchPath":"\"e:/Work/Place_Migrate/cci-scm-web/02_target/CoC.Web.SCM/Controllers\"","toolAction":"\"Searching ApiEndPointOutputCacheData\"","toolSummary":"\"Find ApiEndPointOutputCacheData in Controllers\""}}]}
