{"step_index":7856,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-09T04:27:41Z","thinking":"Initial observation indicates that `BaseController` was originally part of `Core.MVC`. Further investigation is needed to understand the current definition of `Core.MVC.BaseController` in the target project. Checking how the target project describes `Core.MVC.BaseController`.\n\n\nThe original `BaseController` provided session and user context features, including a `CurrentUser` that was supposed to fall back to session data. However, code inspection reveals the target project's `HomeController` does not implement this session fallback mechanism, only casting `User` to `UserPrincipal`. The `ErrorController` suggests a session check, indicating some session use, contradicting the `HomeController`'s direct `User` cast. This discrepancy requires further investigation.\n\n\nThe `ErrorController`'s lack of `HttpContext.User` population and absence of authorization attribute raise concerns about unauthenticated access. This behavior extends beyond the error controller; any action rendering without authorization might exhibit similar issues, including potentially within `ChildActionViewComponent` execution. The core issue lies in inconsistent user context management – specifically, why user population is restricted to a single authorization attribute. Suspecting a more global mechanism, `Startup.cs` will be investigated for user authentication middleware.\n\n\nThe sequence of middleware execution highlights a critical flaw: `UseSession()` runs *after* `UseAuthentication()`, but before authorization. Actions without the authorization attribute, or those reached via exception handling, do not have their `HttpContext.User` populated. This results in a `NullReferenceException` when view components, relying on `CurrentUser`, are invoked in controllers lacking the authorization attribute. The layout or views that invoke these components are also affected. The root cause is a race condition between authentication and authorization, resulting in a missing user context for unauthenticated requests, or exception-handled requests.\n\n\nThe original code's `CurrentUser` property in `HomeController` now correctly includes the session fallback logic, mirroring the fix implemented in `cci-dc-web`. This session fallback compensates for situations where `HttpContext.User` isn't populated, which resolves the `NullReferenceException`. The added session check in `ErrorController` confirms this session-based fallback strategy.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"e:/Work/Place_Migrate/cci-scm-web/02_target/CoC.Web.SCM/Views/Shared/_SiteMaps.cshtml\"","toolAction":"\"Viewing _SiteMaps.cshtml\"","toolSummary":"\"View _SiteMaps.cshtml\""}}]}
