{"step_index":7882,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-09T04:29:39Z","thinking":"Place web uses `UserSessionStore.Load` to retrieve the current user from the session, both in views and controllers. DC web's `BaseController` also utilizes `UserSessionStore.Load` as a fallback when the user isn't already cast as `UserPrincipal`. SCM web's controllers do not inherit.\n\n\nSCM web's `BaseController` uses `UserPrincipal` and sets it through `MvcAuthorizeAttribute`, which populates `HttpContext.User`. The `ErrorController`, crucially, does not employ `MvcAuthorizeAttribute`. Thus, in the `ErrorController`, the `HttpContext.User` remains unset. When `ErrorController.Unauthorized()`'s view renders, it includes the shared layout.\n\n\nThe `CurrentUser` property in `HomeController` is likely returning null because `HttpContext.User` isn't being explicitly set. This leads to null reference exceptions when accessing properties of `CurrentUser` in methods like `Menu` and `LoadUserLogin`. A middleware could be used within `Startup.cs` to populate `context.User` with a user object loaded from session if one exists and `HttpContext.User` is not already a `UserPrincipal`. Changing the `CurrentUser` getter to check the session if the user isn't already set would address the issue. Similar logic should also be applied in the `ErrorController`.\n\n\nMiddleware, if present, ensures `HttpContext.User` is populated for all requests after session middleware runs. This happens even for controllers without authorization attributes, like `ErrorController`.  The `MvcAuthorizeAttribute` still functions correctly, including redirecting to the error page when authorization fails. A redirect to the error controller results in the middleware populating the `HttpContext.User`.\n\n\nThe layout uses `CurrentUser`, which accesses the user through `HomeController`. `HomeController` correctly casts `HttpContext.User` as `UserPrincipal` and renders user-specific information. The layout renders as expected on STG4.\n\nReviewing `HomeController.cs`, the `CurrentUser` property uses a simple cast; incorporating session loading as a fallback strengthens this. Nearly all SCM controllers use a similar approach to retrieve the `CurrentUser`.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"e:/Work/Place_Migrate/cci-dc-web/02_target/CoC.Web.DC/Startup.cs\"","EndLine":"170","StartLine":"120","toolAction":"\"Viewing Startup.cs in DC web\"","toolSummary":"\"View Startup.cs in DC web lines 120-170\""}}]}
