{"step_index":8584,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-12T06:52:40Z","thinking":"The successful token acquisition and MDM permission retrieval are promising, yet the `NullReferenceException` at `UserPrincipal.cs:252` during web app startup requires investigation. The `ClientPermissionBackgroundService`'s `ExecuteAsync` method, which initiates a background task at startup, is the primary area to examine.\n\n\nThe `NullReferenceException` likely occurs because the permission data isn't loaded before the web request accesses it.  The background service loading the permission data might not complete before the initial web request.  Another possible cause could be the `_roleIDs` field within `CurrentUser` being initialized with a null value; the constructor for that class needs careful examination.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"E:/Work/Place_Migrate/cci-scm-web/02_target/CoC.Web.SCM/Permission/UserSessionStore.cs\"","EndLine":"70","StartLine":"1","toolAction":"\"Viewing UserSessionStore.cs\"","toolSummary":"\"View UserSessionStore.cs\""}}]}
{"step_index":8585,"source":"MODEL","type":"VIEW_FILE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-12T06:52:52Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-12T13:52:52+07:00\nCompleted At: 2026-10-12T13:52:52+07:00\nFile Path: `file:///E:/Work/Place_Migrate/cci-scm-web/02_target/CoC.Web.SCM/Permission/UserSessionStore.cs`\nTotal Lines: 77\nTotal Bytes: 3425\nShowing lines 1 to 70\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: //AI[MIGRATE_START]: session persistence of the principal. UserPrincipal derives from ClaimsPrincipal (see UserPrincipal.cs) and Token/RefreshToken carry [JsonIgnore] (playbook §6.4a), so JsonConvert.SerializeObject(user) THROWS PlatformNotSupportedException (\"This instance contains state that cannot be serialized ...\") — reproduced with a harness — which made AccountsController.Callback fail into a /accounts/login loop, and JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<UserPrincipal> could never repopulate the getter-only public properties anyway. The store therefore persists a flat DTO (id_token, refresh_token, the claim list the principal was built from, CFName/DisplayName) under the legacy typo key \"UserPricinpal\" and REBUILDS the principal through the same public ctor on every read (permissions/modules are recomputed from Permission.Data, so they are never stale)\r\n2: using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http;\r\n3: using Newtonsoft.Json;\r\n4: using System.Collections.Generic;\r\n5: using System.Linq;\r\n6: using System.Security.Claims;\r\n7: \r\n8: namespace CoC.Web.SCM\r\n9: {\r\n10:     public static class UserSessionStore\r\n11:     {\r\n12:         public const string PrincipalKey = \"UserPricinpal\";\r\n13: \r\n14:         private sealed class ClaimDto\r\n15:         {\r\n16:             public string Type { get; set; }\r\n17:             public string Value { get; set; }\r\n18:         }\r\n19: \r\n20:         private sealed class SessionDto\r\n21:         {\r\n22:             public string Token { get; set; }\r\n23:             public string RefreshToken { get; set; }\r\n24:             p\n<truncated 185 bytes>\npublic static void Save(ISession session, UserPrincipal user)\r\n30:         {\r\n31:             if (session == null || user == null) return;\r\n32:             var dto = new SessionDto\r\n33:             {\r\n34:                 Token = user.Token,\r\n35:                 RefreshToken = user.RefreshToken,\r\n36:                 Claims = user.Claims.Select(c => new ClaimDto { Type = c.Type, Value = c.Value }).ToList(),\r\n37:                 CFName = user.CFName,\r\n38:                 DisplayName = user.DisplayName\r\n39:             };\r\n40:             session.SetString(PrincipalKey, JsonConvert.SerializeObject(dto));\r\n41:         }\r\n42: \r\n43:         public static void Clear(ISession session)\r\n44:         {\r\n45:             session?.Remove(PrincipalKey);\r\n46:         }\r\n47: \r\n48:         public static bool Exists(ISession session)\r\n49:         {\r\n50:             try { return session != null && !string.IsNullOrEmpty(session.GetString(PrincipalKey)); }\r\n51:             catch { return false; }\r\n52:         }\r\n53: \r\n54:         public static UserPrincipal Load(ISession session)\r\n55:         {\r\n56:             string json;\r\n57:             try { json = session?.GetString(PrincipalKey); } catch { return null; }\r\n58:             if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(json)) return null;\r\n59:             try\r\n60:             {\r\n61:                 var dto = JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<SessionDto>(json);\r\n62:                 if (dto == null || dto.Claims == null) return null;\r\n63:                 var claims = dto.Claims.Select(c => new Claim(c.Type, c.Value ?? string.Empty)).ToList();\r\n64:                 var user = new UserPrincipal(dto.Token, dto.RefreshToken, claims);\r\n65:                 user.CFName = dto.CFName;\r\n66:                 user.DisplayName = dto.DisplayName;\r\n67:                 return user;\r\n68:             }\r\n69:             catch\r\n70:             {\r\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":8586,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-12T06:52:52Z","thinking":"The session deserializes claims on each request, then constructs a `UserPrincipal`. Within the `UserPrincipal` constructor, role IDs are extracted from the claims and deserialized using a library function. This deserialization could be a point of vulnerability if the claims data isn't validated carefully.\n\n\nThe `UserPrincipal` constructor did not crash because the `GetUserPermission` method is wrapped in a `try-catch` block. This handles the potential `ArgumentNullException` that occurs when the `Permission.Data` is null, preventing a crash. However, this issue surfaces later during view rendering when the `IsPermission` method is called.\n\n\nThe `IsPermission` method's vulnerability stems from a lack of null checks on `Permission.Data`, `Permission.Data.Permission`, and `_roleIDs` before accessing their members. The method does not handle cases where these are null, or when `RoleFunctionName` is null or empty, resulting in a `NullReferenceException`. Robustness requires comprehensive null checks to prevent the exception.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"grep_search","args":{"CaseInsensitive":"false","MatchPerLine":"true","Query":"\"Permission.Data\"","SearchPath":"\"E:/Work/Place_Migrate/cci-scm-web/02_target/CoC.Web.SCM\"","toolAction":"\"Searching all Permission.Data usages\"","toolSummary":"\"Search Permission.Data usages in target\""}}]}
