{"step_index":9092,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-13T06:58:27Z","thinking":"The code snippet focuses on setting the bearer token for API requests using `HttpClient`. Examining `ApiHelper.cs` reveals the token is derived from `CurrentUser`. The `HttpClientExtensions` class defines the `SetBearerToken` method which utilizes `SetToken` to add the `Authorization` header to outgoing HTTP requests. This suggests the token is crucial for authenticating API calls.\n\n\nThe critical difference is how the `Authorization` header is handled when the token is null or empty. The original implementation sets an empty bearer token, preventing a null reference exception on the API side because the header is still present. The change now conditionally sets the header, leading to its omission when the token is missing, triggering a crash on the API side when the missing header is accessed. Additionally, the root cause is a potential bug with how the token is initialized, leading to possible null or empty values. The API side is vulnerable as it doesn't check if `Headers[\"Authorization\"] == null`.\n\n\nThe code directly accesses the `Authorization` header without a null check, leading to a potential `NullReferenceException` if the header is missing. The original code's `SetBearer` method sets an empty \"Bearer\" value when the token is null/empty to prevent this. The root cause for the missing token could be due to a failed login, session issue, or token acquisition failure.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"git show dev:02_target/net10_mssql/CoC.Business.Service.SCM/Helper/ApiHelper/ApiHelper.cs | Select-String \\\"Authorization\\\" -Context 2,2\"","Cwd":"\"e:\\\\Work\\\\Place_Migrate\\\\cci-scm-api\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Checking ApiHelper on dev branch of cci-scm-api\"","toolSummary":"\"Check ApiHelper on dev branch\""}}]}
