{"step_index":9311,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-13T07:42:38Z","thinking":"The `ErrorRes` structure contains only an `ErrorMessage` property, which contrasts with the ASP.NET Web API's 500 error JSON payload that includes \"Message\", \"ExceptionMessage\", \"ExceptionType\", and \"StackTrace\".  This discrepancy implies that when an API 500 error occurs, the `ResponseError` object's `ErrorMessage` will be null. Consequently, the `SetErrorAPI` method evaluates the `ResponseError`'s `ErrorMessage` as null, leading to an incorrect result.\n\n\nIt seems that if the HTTP content stream isn't buffered before being read, subsequent attempts to read it in `getRawContent` might fail. This is because `ReadAsStringAsync().Result` consumes the entire stream. Buffering the content using the specified function at the start of the error handling will ensure multiple reads are possible. The error message is then constructed from either the provided error details or a default status code.\n\n\nThe current error handling masks the underlying exception details. The logger, without configured sinks in the local environment, fails to persist any useful diagnostic information. The exception thrown provides minimal detail, hindering debugging.\n\nA solution involves writing the complete error information to a local file for local debugging. Candidate file paths are explored, considering both the project root directory and the application base directory to maximize discoverability.\n\n\nComprehensive logging is required for both failed and, optionally, successful API calls, to facilitate debugging and monitoring.  For failures, specific data, including timestamps, user details, tokens, endpoint information, HTTP status, request/response content, and error messages, must be recorded.  Successful calls should be logged if a debug flag is enabled or if the user desires more detailed insights.\n\n\nEnsuring `LoadIntoBufferAsync()` is called at